 # 架构总览
 
+## BioRouter downstream runtime contract
+
+The BioRouter patch adds `screen_capture` as the tenth tool to all three runtimes. It accepts optional `display` (zero-based topology index), `window_title` (one case-insensitive substring match), and `list_only` (default false). Display and window selection are mutually exclusive. Omitted selection captures the primary display. Listing returns JSON topology text; capture adds a PNG image content block. macOS uses ScreenCaptureKit; Windows uses DPI-aware Win32 screen pixels; Linux uses distro Python GI/GDK on X11. Windows and Linux window captures include occluding content. Wayland lists topology but rejects pixel capture until a consented portal backend is implemented.
+
+The macOS app agent requires an installation/version socket namespace, verifies the exact peer bundle and executable, and never terminates a foreign agent. Each socket connection owns its MCP server and snapshots. Every operation, including empty environment overrides, holds the shared environment lock; omitted OCU environment keys are cleared within that scope. Overlay cleanup is synchronous under that same owner lock; ambient distributed turn-ended listeners are removed. Disconnected owners cancel input between chunks/gesture steps and cancel screenshot waits. AX calls have a bounded messaging timeout; ambiguous completion errors stop alternate-path replay. A delivered OS action cannot be undone. New connections require `get_app_state` before actions. BioRouter must terminate the proxy on Stop, discard late results, manage process trees and temporary operation files on Windows/Linux, and replace connections on desktop ownership handoff.
+
+Windows initializes framework UIA client-side providers from a compiled, non-inlined C# caller before any PowerShell UIA request. This avoids the default provider loader walking a PowerShell dynamic frame with a null reflected type. The UIA bridge runs in an MTA; its per-operation process does not own application windows. Provider loading failures remain errors, rather than silently presenting generic HWND panes as full accessibility.
+
+Linux scroll focuses the requested accessibility component and then emits symbolic page keys. Key symbols use `ATSPI_KEY_SYM`; modifier down/up events use hardware keycodes resolved from the desktop keymap and release in a finally block. Failed target focus or rejected input is an explicit tool error. The former `PRESSRELEASE` keysym path silently delivered no scroll on GTK and is removed.
+
+`doctor --json` provides passive JSON readiness without screenshots, app enumeration, or onboarding. It reports `state`, `desktop_available`, `accessibility`, `screen_recording`, `capture_available`, and `message`. Bare `doctor` retains upstream interactive onboarding behavior; hosts must not use it for polling. Cache passive results to avoid repeatedly launching platform bridges.
+
+macOS snapshots associate the AX root with a unique on-screen window using its geometry, with title as an ambiguity tie-breaker. They never substitute an overlapping sibling window for the screenshot. Authorized global pointer input activates the app and raises that captured AX window, then checks fresh system-wide AX foreground identity and unchanged CG window ownership/geometry before delivery. Missing, ambiguous, changed, or unfocusable windows require a new `get_app_state`; preparation waits are bounded and cancellation-aware.
+
+With global input consent, macOS `type_text` and `press_key` prepare the exact captured window and refresh its focused state without activation recovery before acting. Without that consent, they retain targeted-only behavior. `type_text` requires a text role or a settable control described as text entry before either AX value mutation or keyboard delivery; a generic settable container is not an editable text target.
+
+Focused AX elements are filtered by application PID and owning AX window before their selected text or summaries are read. Keyboard delivery and typing value mutations recheck current target focus without activation; consented foreground input also requires the same current foreground application and window. Focus changes during screenshot capture therefore cannot authorize a foreign target.
+
+macOS `set_value` reads the current AX scalar type before mutation: string controls receive literal strings, numeric controls receive finite CFNumber values, and boolean controls accept only `true` or `false`. Unsupported types or invalid values fail before input. An uncertain AX write outcome is not retried.
+
+Windows drag requires `OPEN_COMPUTER_USE_ALLOW_GLOBAL_POINTER_FALLBACKS=1`; absent or disabled consent returns unsupported before focus or input. It resolves the start child under the selected root and verifies root/child ownership, unchanged snapshot geometry, foreground identity, unheld mouse buttons, and every planned point's visible owning window. One `SendInput` batch contains absolute virtual-desktop motion, down, noncoalesced drag moves, and up. This preserves native button state for controls such as WinForms and keeps the complete gesture inside one native call rather than holding a button across script sleeps. A blocked or partial batch is an error, never replayed; partial insertion attempts only a movement-free button release. Already inserted input cannot be cancelled. Drag paths into another window or through an occluding window are refused.
+
+A successful macOS AX write is not sufficient evidence of a value change: `set_value` polls the same control for the requested scalar and otherwise returns an unconfirmed-outcome error without another mutation. Chromium intentionally rejects direct slider setters in [its AX implementation](https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/HEAD/ui/accessibility/platform/ax_platform_node_cocoa.mm); use the slider's exposed Increment/Decrement actions or a deliberate drag when direct setting is unavailable. Integral AX page scrolling observes target-local descendant geometry and exposed axis scrollbar values, then waits for movement to settle before sending the next requested page. A direct target-owned axis scrollbar with finite min/max can prove a reached boundary; the result reports the actual verified page count. Calls accept up to 100 pages to bound work and avoid numeric conversion overflow. Unobserved progress stops remaining actions and reports the verified page count; it is not silently retried. Neither polling path changes keyboard or pointer event recipes.
+
+The macOS wheel fallback also sequences requested pages through the progress observer. It uses pixel-unit events sized to the visible target viewport, one page per event plus any fractional remainder. The former single line-unit event (`12 * pages`) let applications cap a multi-page request to one viewport; neither global nor targeted wheel dispatch uses that encoding now.
+
+Numeric action defaults apply only when the argument is absent. Provided `pages` must be a finite number in `(0, 100]`; `click_count` must be a whole number in `[1, 100]`. Booleans, nulls, strings, nonfinite values, and out-of-range counts fail before action dispatch. Tool schemas publish these bounds; repeated clicks retain cancellation checks between actions.
+
+The remaining sections describe the pinned upstream baseline; BioRouter-specific behavior above takes precedence.
+
