+# BioRouter native capture and connection isolation
+
+Request: ship a pinned native computer-use helper with display/window capture and per-chat observation state, replacing host capture/script fallbacks.
+
+Changes: added a tenth screen_capture tool, native platform capture/topology bridges, strict target argument validation, image-block preservation, macOS disconnect cancellation and bounded AX calls, serialized owner-scoped environment/overlay lifetime, strict app-agent identity checking, and explicit state-before-action enforcement. Linux invokes distro /usr/bin/python3 for packaged GI dependencies. SkyLight input is explicitly unsupported in the BioRouter build.
+
+Validation: Python bridge tests cover topology, image bytes, missing GDK, missing selection and Wayland rejection. Swift contract tests cover malformed capture arguments, connection-local initial state, cancellation scope and screenshot-wait cancellation. Go tests cover the ten-tool roster and capture schema/argument rejection. Interactive macOS/Windows/X11 capture and signed artifact checks remain separate release gates; Wayland pixel capture is not implemented.
+
+Verified locally: Swift native app builds with one worker; 5 new contract tests and 163 existing unit regressions pass. Linux and Windows Go unit suites pass on macOS, with one worker. Twelve Python bridge tests pass. The built Swift binary completes MCP initialization and advertises exactly ten tools. Passive doctor JSON parsing and explicit no-state errors pass. These checks do not establish interactive Windows/Linux behavior or signed-helper TCC identity.
+
+Follow-up real Linux ARM64 GTK/X11 fixture exposed no-op scroll despite tool success. The bridge incorrectly supplied keysyms to hardware-key synthesis and ignored the requested scroll element. It now focuses that element, uses symbolic key synthesis, resolves modifier hardware codes from the actual keymap, rejects failed delivery, and releases held modifiers on failure. The unchanged independent GTK adjustment assertion now passes, alongside text/click state and nonblank native PNG capture; explicit Wayland capture rejection still passes. This is container X11 evidence, not native GNOME/KDE Wayland validation.
+
+Windows CI exposed a real WinForms fixture only as generic HWND panes, with AccessibleName and control patterns missing. Microsoft UIA ProxyManager.LoadDefaultProxies sets its one-shot load flag before walking managed stack frames and dereferences MethodBase.ReflectedType. A PowerShell dynamic frame can leave provider initialization incomplete. The bridge now initializes the framework provider table through a compiled, non-inlined caller and runs on MTA. This diagnosis requires a passing real Windows fixture before acceptance; the fixture assertions remain unchanged. Primary source: https://github.com/dotnet/wpf/blob/main/src/Microsoft.DotNet.Wpf/src/UIAutomation/UIAutomationClient/MS/Internal/Automation/ProxyManager.cs (LoadDefaultProxies and RegisterWindowHandlers).
+
+Windows scroll follow-up: WM_MOUSEWHEEL and WM_MOUSEHWHEEL carry screen coordinates, while the upstream fallback converted them to client coordinates. Horizontal wheel delta must be positive for right and negative for left. The bridge now preserves the target's screen point, corrects the horizontal sign, and rejects failed PostMessage delivery. The independent Windows fixture uses a nonzero window origin, measures incoming wheel coordinates before default processing, and checks both scroll offsets. A passing interactive run is still required. Primary documentation: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/inputdev/wm-mousewheel and https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/inputdev/wm-mousehwheel.
+
+The next Windows fixture independently confirmed vertical scrolling but caught a second coordinate defect: the Go scroll request omitted WindowBounds, so Get-ScreenPoint returned null and PowerShell coerced both integer coordinates to zero. Scroll now forwards the observed window bounds like click and drag; missing geometry raises an actionable error instead of targeting screen origin. A request-level regression exercises serialized positive and negative desktop origins through the real service scroll path.
+
+Windows candidate5 confirmed correct wheel delivery and screen coordinates, but standard WinForms ignored horizontal wheel messages. Before dispatching horizontal input, the runtime now selects the nearest horizontally scrollable UIA ancestor within the selected process/window. Vertical behavior remains unchanged. Selection occurs before mutation; provider exceptions propagate without retrying another ancestor or sending wheel input. Windows-only provider contract tests cover nearest ancestor selection, process/window boundaries, unchanged vertical fallback, and no replay after an ambiguous exception. The interactive fixture continues to require an independently measured positive horizontal offset. API documentation: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.windows.automation.scrollpattern.scroll and https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.windows.automation.treewalker.getparent.
+
+On macOS 26.6.2, final nested-app validation showed Accessibility attributed to org.biorouter.computer-use while Screen Recording was attributed to the enclosing com.electron.biorouter bundle. The passive runtime check correctly rejected missing outer-app permission. Diagnostics and capture-denial guidance now name the helper for Accessibility and the outermost enclosing application for Screen Recording; standalone helpers retain one permission owner. Only valid application bundles are used to resolve the display name. Both CGPreflightScreenCaptureAccess guards remain unchanged. Focused tests cover standalone, multiply nested, and non-application directory paths.
+
+## Captured-window input association
+
+Preserved the snapshot AX window alongside its CG identity, removed capture substitution by overlapping sibling windows, and bound global pointer preparation to that captured target. Preparation validates ownership and geometry before and after activation/raise, checks current system-wide AX focus, and refuses changed or ambiguous targets. Input event timing is unchanged. Six focused Swift tests cover capture matching, ambiguity, ordering, stale identity/geometry, failure, timeout, and cancellation. Live drag acceptance remains separate.
+
+Cancellation is rechecked after the potentially blocking initial AX validation and readiness query. A callback regression first failed on the unfixed implementation (activation after cancellation and delivery after a cancelled readiness query), then passed after the checks. The final non-live Swift suite passes 179 tests; real-input tests are excluded.
+
+## Typing into an initially inactive application
+
+Actual BioRouter testing found that a settable root container could be misclassified as a text target. The type_text eligibility check now precedes both AX value writes and keyboard fallback, rejecting non-text containers while preserving text fields and described custom editors. With global input consent, typing and key presses prepare the captured window and refresh focus; targeted-only calls do not activate the app. The new container regression failed on the old implementation (four role cases), then passed; four focused and 180 non-live tests pass. Live verification of the new payload remains required.
+
+Focused element ownership is now checked before reading selected text, covering both foreign applications and other windows of the same application. Fresh nonactivating focus checks precede typing mutation and key delivery. Removing ownership checks deliberately fails one regression with four assertions; the restored non-live suite passes 181 tests.
+
+## Scalar AX value writes
+
+A live numeric slider accepted a string-typed AX write without changing value. set_value now preserves the current AX scalar category: literal CFString, finite CFNumber, or strict CFBoolean. Invalid input and unsupported current types fail before mutation; cancellation is checked around the type read and before the write, with no replay after uncertain completion. Four conversion tests fail against the old string-only conversion and pass after the fix; the full non-live suite passes 185 tests. Scroll delivery remains unchanged pending foreground retesting.
+
+## Windows native drag delivery
+
+The independent Windows child fixture received no drag events despite a successful tool acknowledgment. Root-HWND PostMessage delivery missed the child, and WinForms derives MouseMove button state from the input queue rather than posted message flags. Windows drag therefore now requires explicit global-pointer consent and uses one bounded native SendInput batch, including noncoalesced moves and release. Before delivery it validates the selected root/child ownership, unchanged snapshot geometry, foreground identity, unheld mouse buttons, and each planned point's visible owning window. Unsupported background drag fails explicitly. Partial insertion attempts movement-free release only and never replays the gesture; already inserted input cannot be cancelled.
+
+The PowerShell contract covers disabled consent without focus/input, nested child coordinate resolution, foreign process/root/sibling rejection, failed translation, out-of-window points, bounded traversal, hidden/minimized/changed windows, foreground failure/change, held buttons, occlusion, virtual-desktop normalization, complete batch ordering, and blocked/partial insertion. Script parsing, native C# declaration compilation and INPUT ABI checks, and the mocked contract pass in portable PowerShell. Deliberate consent-bypass and moving-release regressions fail. Actual Windows fixture validation remains required; its independent pressed-motion/displacement assertions are unchanged, with global-pointer consent enabled only in the fixture helper environment.
+
+API references: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winuser/nf-winuser-sendinput, https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winuser/ns-winuser-mouseinput, and https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winuser/nf-winuser-childwindowfrompointex.
+
+## Verified scalar writes and sequential page scrolling
+
+Live fixture events prove page scrolling moved, but rapid repeated AX page requests were coalesced into one viewport per multi-page call. Added bounded target-local geometry observation between authorized page actions; missing progress reports partial/unconfirmed completion without sending remaining actions. Scalar setters now verify readback instead of treating acknowledgment as success; Chromium slider direct writes can be rejected by the application, so alternatives remain explicit user/agent choices. Nine targeted regressions fail under old-behavior mutations (21 assertions) and pass restored; full non-live suite passes 194 tests. Updated payload live verification remains required.
+
+Review additions cover fixed-frame controls through axis scrollbar values, finite page bounds, and explicit edge reporting only from a direct target-owned axis scrollbar with valid min/max. Ancestor progress sampling stops at the application/window boundary. The final non-live suite passes 198 tests; all four new source/test files are included in the patch diff via intent-to-add.
+
+## Wheel fallback page semantics
+
+The next live run showed that the browser HTML-content node exposed no AXByPage action: it used the still-unmodified wheel fallback. Its single line-unit event was capped at one viewport even for pages=3. The fallback now sends one viewport-sized pixel event per authorized page and a fractional final event, observing progress between them. Tests inspect real constructed CGEvent pixel deltas without posting input, and cover horizontal axes and oversized-content clipping. The old single-event/line-unit mutation fails two tests with four assertions; restored non-live suite passes 200 tests. Live acceptance remains required.
+
+## Strict numeric action parsing
+
+Removed silent fallback for provided invalid pages/click_count and rejected Boolean bridging, nonfinite numbers, fractional click counts, and unsafe integer conversion. Both counts default only when absent; clicks are bounded to 1–100 and pages to greater than zero through 100, with matching tool schema bounds. Existing global, targeted, and AX repeated-click loops check cancellation between actions. The old invalid-type behavior failed one regression with 11 assertions; the restored non-live suite passes 205 tests, including schema and deserialized Boolean/null checks.
